index 0000000..6e93aa8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/features/link-expiry.md
@@ -0,0 +1,162 @@
+# Link Expiry
+
+Short links stop working a fixed number of days after they are created. This keeps stale links from staying live forever.
+
+## How it works
+
+- Every link has an expiry time:
+
+  `expiresAt = createdAt + N × 24 hours`
+
+  where **N** is the configured `shortener.link.expiry-days`. The default is 30.
+- A link counts as **expired** once the current time is **at or after** `expiresAt`.
+  - With N = 7, a link created at `2026-10-14T10:00:00Z` still redirects at `2026-10-21T09:59:59Z`.
+  - From `2026-10-21T10:00:00Z` onward it returns `410 Gone`.
+- Expiry is checked each time a link is followed. It is not stored per link. It is always calculated from `createdAt` and the current value of N.
+- **Expired links are not deleted.**
+  - They stay in storage.
+  - Their statistics stay available.
+  - Their short codes are never given to new links.
+- **Expired requests are not counted as clicks.** Following an expired link does not change `clickCount` or `lastAccessedAt`.
+- N is a single global setting. Callers cannot set expiry for an individual link, and links cannot be marked "never expire".
+
+## API changes
+
+### `GET /{code}`: follow a short link
+
+| Situation | Status | Notes |
+|---|---|---|
+| Link is active (`now < expiresAt`) | `302 Found` | `Location: <originalUrl>`. Unchanged. |
+| Link has expired (`now >= expiresAt`) | `410 Gone` | **No `Location` header.** The body is a problem detail (`application/problem+json`). |
+| Code was never created | `404 Not Found` | Unchanged. |
+
+Active link:
+
+```bash
+curl -i http://localhost:8080/Ab3dE9x
+```
+
+```http
+HTTP/1.1 302 Found
+Location: https://example.com/some/long/path
+```
+
+Expired link:
+
+```bash
+curl -i http://localhost:8080/Ab3dE9x
+```
+
+```http
+HTTP/1.1 410 Gone
+Content-Type: application/problem+json
+
+{
+  "type": "about:blank",
+  "title": "Gone",
+  "status": 410,
+  "detail": "Short link for code 'Ab3dE9x' has expired",
+  "instance": "/Ab3dE9x"
+}
+```
+
+### `POST /shorten`: create a short link
+
+The response body has a new field, `expiresAt`. Nothing else changes:
+
+- the status is still `201`
+- the `Location` header is the same
+- the `400` and `429` responses are the same
+- all existing fields are the same
+
+```bash
+curl -i -X POST http://localhost:8080/shorten \
+  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
+  -d '{"url": "https://example.com/some/long/path"}'
+```
+
+```http
+HTTP/1.1 201 Created
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+{
+  "code": "Ab3dE9x",
+  "shortUrl": "http://localhost:8080/Ab3dE9x",
+  "originalUrl": "https://example.com/some/long/path",
+  "expiresAt": "2026-11-13T10:00:00Z"
+}
+```
+
+This example assumes the default of N = 30 and a link created at `2026-10-14T10:00:00Z`.
+
+### `GET /stats/{code}`: link statistics
+
+- The response body has a new field, `expiresAt`, after `lastAccessedAt`.
+- **This endpoint still returns `200` for expired links**, so you can check whether a link has expired and when.
+- Unknown codes still return `404`.
+
+```bash
+curl -s http://localhost:8080/stats/Ab3dE9x
+```
+
+```json
+{
+  "code": "Ab3dE9x",
+  "originalUrl": "https://example.com/some/long/path",
+  "clickCount": 3,
+  "createdAt": "2026-10-14T10:00:00Z",
+  "lastAccessedAt": "2026-10-15T09:00:00Z",
+  "expiresAt": "2026-11-13T10:00:00Z"
+}
+```
+
+### `expiresAt` format
+
+- It is an ISO-8601 UTC timestamp ending in `Z`, for example `2026-11-13T10:00:00Z`.
+- Fractional seconds appear only when they are non-zero, with at most 6 digits. For example: `2026-11-13T10:00:00.123456Z`.
+- It always equals `createdAt` plus N × 24 hours.
+
+## Error cases
+
+| Case | Result |
+|---|---|
+| Following an expired link | `410 Gone`, problem-detail body, no `Location`, click not counted |
+| Following an unknown code | `404 Not Found`, unchanged from before |
+| Stats for an expired link | `200 OK` with `expiresAt` in the past |
+| `shortener.link.expiry-days` set to `0` or a negative number | The application **fails to start**. The error includes `shortener.link.expiry-days must be at least 1, but was <value>`. |
+| `shortener.link.expiry-days` set to a non-number (e.g. `abc`) | The application **fails to start**. The error includes `Failed to bind properties under 'shortener.link.expiry-days' to int`. |
+
+## Configuration
+
+| Property | Type | Default | Constraint |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `shortener.link.expiry-days` | integer (days) | `30` | must be ≥ 1 |
+
+Example in `application.properties`:
+
+```properties
+# Days after creation until a link returns 410 Gone (minimum 1, default 30).
+shortener.link.expiry-days=7
+```
+
+Or set it with an environment variable or command-line argument, using Spring Boot's standard binding:
+
+```bash
+SHORTENER_LINK_EXPIRY_DAYS=7 java -jar shortener.jar
+java -jar shortener.jar --shortener.link.expiry-days=7
+```
+
+The value is read at startup. Changes take effect after a restart.
+
+## Upgrade and migration notes
+
+- **No database migration.** The schema is unchanged. Expiry uses the `createdAt` that is already stored for every link.
+- **Existing links expire retroactively.**
+  - On the first deploy, any existing link whose `createdAt` is more than N days old starts returning `410 Gone` straight away.
+  - Links younger than N days keep redirecting until they reach `expiresAt`.
+  - **This is pending human approval before release.** Check how many live links are older than N days before you deploy.
+- **Changing N affects every link, old and new.** Each restart re-evaluates all links against the new value:
+  - **Lowering N** can expire links that were previously active.
+  - **Raising N** brings previously expired links back to life, as long as they are younger than the new N.
+- **Client compatibility.** `POST /shorten` and `GET /stats/{code}` now return an extra `expiresAt` field. Clients that reject unknown JSON fields must be updated.
+- **Clients that follow redirects** should treat `410 Gone` as "this link existed but has expired", which is different from `404 Not Found` ("this link never existed").
